"""Optional evidence investigation cells; no fixture authoring or scoring workflow."""
EXERCISES = {
'05_Investigate_alerts': [
('Run the scripted evidence checker',
 "Choose an alert from your current rule. This checker follows a fixed Python sequence to retrieve the grant, subsequent subject activity, approvals and inventory. It makes no LLM calls. Inspect its returned rows and severity reasons; its hard-coded assessment is a suggestion to review, not proof of compromise. The import `from agent.triage import run` points to the shared Python module: calling `run` without `model` selects this scripted mode.",
 'from workshop.exercises import artifacts, save_records\nfrom agent.triage import run\nimport pandas as pd\nsession.refresh_rule()\nalerts = session.execute("SELECT DISTINCT alert_key FROM main.workshop_alerts ORDER BY 1")\n# TODO: keep the first alert, or uncomment the next line and paste another alert key.\nselected_alert_key = alerts[0]["alert_key"] if alerts else None\n# selected_alert_key = "COPY_ALERT_KEY"\nif selected_alert_key:\n    own_record = run(session.engine, selected_alert_key, split="enterprise")\n    save_records("my-notebook-alert.jsonl", [own_record])\n    display(own_record["assessment"])\n    display(pd.DataFrame(own_record["trace"]))\nelse:\n    print("Your rule produced no alerts. Review the source activity and rule conditions.")'),
('Optionally run an AI investigator with a local model',
 'This optional mode uses an LLM to choose evidence tools and write an assessment. Passing `model=LOCAL_MODEL` to `run` selects this mode. Inspect every claim and returned event; there is no ground-truth score in this workshop.\n\n### Install and start Ollama\n\nRun these commands in a **terminal**, on the same machine as the Jupyter kernel.\n\n1. Install Ollama from [ollama.com/download](https://ollama.com/download). On macOS or Windows, open the installed Ollama application. On Linux, install it with:\n   ```bash\n   curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh\n   ```\n2. If Ollama is not already running as an application or service, start it and leave this terminal open:\n   ```bash\n   ollama serve\n   ```\n   If it reports that port 11434 is already in use, Ollama may already be running. Check with `ollama list` in another terminal.\n3. In another terminal, download the model:\n   ```bash\n   ollama pull qwen3:8b\n   ```\n\n### Run the AI investigator\n\nRun the **scripted evidence checker** cell above first; it imports `run` and selects `selected_alert_key`. Then change the settings in the next cell to:\n\n```python\nRUN_LOCAL_MODEL = True\nLOCAL_MODEL = "qwen3:8b"\n```\n\nRun that cell with **Shift+Enter**. It connects to Ollama at `http://localhost:11434`, displays the assessment and tool trace, and saves the record to `artifacts/my-local-model-alert.jsonl`. No API key is needed. The initial model download requires internet access; model execution may take several minutes, depending on your hardware.\n\nLeave `RUN_LOCAL_MODEL = False` to skip the AI investigator and use only the scripted checker.',
 '# TODO: enable local inference only after installing Ollama and downloading this model.\nRUN_LOCAL_MODEL = False\nLOCAL_MODEL = "qwen3:8b"\nif RUN_LOCAL_MODEL and selected_alert_key:\n    model_record = run(session.engine, selected_alert_key, split="enterprise", model=LOCAL_MODEL)\n    save_records("my-local-model-alert.jsonl", [model_record])\n    display(model_record["assessment"])\n    display(pd.DataFrame(model_record["trace"]))\nelse:\n    print("Local inference skipped. Enable RUN_LOCAL_MODEL after installing Ollama.")'),
('Compare raw and normalized evidence with the same AI investigator', "**Question:** Does a shared schema reduce the translation work an LLM must do to investigate the same behavior?\n\nThis is an optional extension after the one-hour core workshop. You run it yourself; no facilitator or saved model answers are required. Complete the Ollama setup above first. Allow several minutes per run, and potentially much longer on a CPU.\n\n| Setting | Raw run | Normalized run |\n|---|---|---|\n| Task | Investigate the selected grant and cite supporting events | Same task and grant |\n| Evidence | `raw_eks`, `raw_gke`, `raw_aks`, with provider-specific JSON | One `events` table with consistent event fields and request data |\n| Tools | `list_tables`, `query_sql`, `submit_assessment` | Identical tools |\n| Model, prompt, turn limit | Your local model, same investigation prompt, 12 turns | Identical settings, fresh conversation |\n| Supporting context | Identity, inventory and approval tables | Same context |\n\nGuided lookup tools would hide schema differences, so **both runs use SQL-only tools**. The normalized snapshot comes from the fuller prepared event schema, not the small binding-only table you built in notebook 02. Normalized request subjects remain in request data in this snapshot; the model may still need JSON extraction.\n\n### Run your first pair\n\n1. Run setup and the scripted checker above to define `session` and `selected_alert_key`.\n2. In the next cell, set `RUN_SCHEMA_COMPARISON = True` and use the model you downloaded. You can skip the earlier single AI run; this comparison makes its own model calls.\n3. Run the cell. It starts **two fresh investigations of the same grant**, raw then normalized. Progress appears before each run. Each completed result or error is saved to `artifacts/raw-vs-normalized.jsonl`; rerunning replaces that file.\n4. Read the comparison table and the query/assessment output below it. `submitted` means the model returned an assessment, not that its answer is correct. `incomplete` means it exhausted the turn limit; `error` usually means the local service or model request failed.\n\nReference-event coverage is calculated **after** each run using your rule's supporting events; these reference IDs are not shown to the model. Coverage is a navigation aid, not a correctness score. Check cited event contents, asset scope, recipient, success and the 15-minute window yourself. A model may retrieve an event without reasoning about it correctly.\n\n### Compare what the model had to do\n\n- Did both assessments cite the grant and a valid secret-list event? Did either cite events it never retrieved?\n- In the raw queries, find provider table choices, JSON paths and native status checks. What replaces them in the normalized queries? Tool errors count SQL/tool failures, not silent reasoning mistakes.\n- Which run needed fewer SQL queries or corrections? Compare token usage only after checking the evidence.\n- Is the conclusion stronger than the logs support? Secret-list metadata alone does not prove exfiltration or malicious intent.\n\nTo explore all three clouds, set `COMPARE_ALL_CLOUDS = True` and rerun. This chooses one alert per provider present in your rule, for **up to six model runs**. Each pair uses the same alert; it does not merge cloud activity or prove cross-cloud attribution. A single-provider pair demonstrates field translation; the three-provider extension shows how those translation differences repeat.\n\nResults may favor either representation. Timing excludes snapshot creation but includes model/tool work and model loading. Raw runs first, so model warm-up can affect timing; repeat in the opposite order with `NORMALIZED_FIRST = True` before drawing timing conclusions. One small experiment does not establish a general performance advantage.\n\n**Finish:** Write two sentences describing a query difference you observed and whether both answers were supported by evidence. If both runs fail, inspect the errors, verify `ollama list`, and try again with the same settings for both runs. Do not interpret failure as evidence that one schema is better.", 'from workshop.agent_comparison import compare_evidence, comparison_rows\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom IPython.display import display\n\n# TODO: install/start Ollama and pull this model using the instructions above.\nRUN_SCHEMA_COMPARISON = False\nCOMPARISON_MODEL = "qwen3:8b"\nCOMPARE_ALL_CLOUDS = False\nNORMALIZED_FIRST = False\n\nif RUN_SCHEMA_COMPARISON:\n    comparison_records = compare_evidence(\n        session, selected_alert_key, COMPARISON_MODEL,\n        all_clouds=COMPARE_ALL_CLOUDS, max_turns=12,\n        normalized_first=NORMALIZED_FIRST,\n    )\n    display(pd.DataFrame(comparison_rows(comparison_records)))\n    for record in comparison_records:\n        print("\\n", record["comparison_target"], record["representation"])\n        if record.get("error"):\n            print(record["error"])\n        for step in record["trace"]:\n            if step["tool"] == "query_sql":\n                print("SQL:", step["input"].get("sql"))\n                if step["is_error"]:\n                    print("ERROR:", step["result"])\n        display(pd.DataFrame(record["trace"]))\n        display(record.get("assessment") or "No assessment submitted; inspect the saved trace.")\nelse:\n    print("Comparison skipped. Set RUN_SCHEMA_COMPARISON=True after the Ollama setup.")'),
]}

EVIDENCE_REVIEW = "## Explain why this alert fired — 3 minutes\n\nBefore running the scripted checker or AI investigator, use the **source-event timeline above** to decide whether this alert matches the rule you wrote. Your goal is to explain the observed behavior, not to declare an incident.\n\n### Check the two sides of the sequence\n\n| Check | Evidence to find |\n|---|---|\n| Access was granted | A successful `create` of a `clusterrolebindings` resource, with `role_kind = 'ClusterRole'`, `role = 'cluster-admin'` and the recipient listed as a User in the request subjects |\n| The recipient used that access | A successful `list` of `secrets` by that recipient, with `namespace IS NULL` for a cluster-wide request |\n| The events belong together | The same provider, account and cluster; the secret list occurs at or after the grant, within 15 minutes |\n\nThe actor creating the binding may differ from the recipient. Follow the **recipient** when checking the secret-list event. Use `status_id = 1` to verify success. Copy the event IDs so someone else can check your finding.\n\n### Write your finding\n\nAdd a **Markdown cell** below this section (press Escape, then B to insert a cell, then M to make it Markdown). Fill in these five lines using your selected alert:\n\n```text\nGrant event ID: ...\nSecret-list event ID: ...\nWhy the rule matched: [actor] granted [recipient] cluster-admin on [provider/account/cluster] at [time]; [recipient] listed secrets at [time], [N] minutes later.\nPossible legitimate explanation: ...\nBefore escalating, I would check: ...\n```\n\nFor the last two lines, consider whether this was expected administrative work and which ticket, owner confirmation or additional logs would help establish that. The exercise data does not supply validated approvals; an empty approvals table does not prove the work was unauthorized.\n\n**You are done when:** your finding cites both events and explains the recipient, asset scope, success and time window. If you cannot find a required event, say which evidence is missing instead of filling the gap with an assumption. A successful secret-list request alone does not establish that secrets were exfiltrated.\n\nNext, run the scripted checker and compare its claims with your own finding. Check any disagreement against the events; do not treat its disposition as the answer key."
